description:
Context: Users and downstream SDKs reported friction with the existing stream APIs and auth handshake coupling (token exchange logic embedded in transport layers). This PR introduces a coordinated cross-SDK change: 1) rename the streaming surface from `stream()` to `subscribe()` to better reflect push/update semantics and to align ergonomics across Python/TypeScript/Go SDKs, 2) extract an explicit CredentialProvider interface that decouples token lifecycle (refresh, preflight, lease) from transport, enabling safer retries and clearer ownership, 3) introduce small retry-profile presets tuned for streaming (latency-first, throughput-first) and expose them consistently across SDKs, and 4) bump package versions and update docs and examples. This PR is intended to be backward-compatible via a deprecation shim: existing `stream()` calls still work but emit a deprecation warning and delegate to the new subscribe facade. Motivation: Having both API and auth semantics diverge across languages increased cognitive load for integrators and made cross-language examples error-prone. By defining a minimal CredentialProvider contract (get_token, refresh_if_needed, lease_ttl_hint) and an ergonomic subscribe async iterator / async generator / channel abstraction per-language, we simplify patterns for reconnects, token rotation, and deterministic shutdown. Checklist: - [x] API design review and signature lock (ENG-4321) - [x] TS implementation + unit/integration tests - [x] Py implementation + unit/integration tests - [x] Go implementation + unit/integration tests - [x] Docs + examples updated - [x] Backwards-compat shim and deprecation notice - [x] Release notes drafted Summary of commits: - Extract credential provider abstraction and small test harness - Implement subscribe facade in TypeScript (async generator + convenience helper) - Implement subscribe facade in Python (async iterator + context manager) - Implement subscribe facade in Go (channel-based subscriber + Close semantics) - Add retry profile presets and wire to subscribe plumbing - Add deprecation shim for legacy stream() callsites and tests - Docs: streaming.md rewrite + examples/subscribe demos - Changelog bumps: py 1.31.0, ts 1.27.0, go 1.8.1 High-level changed areas/files (representative, not exhaustive): - sdk/typescript/src/subscribe/index.ts (new facade + docs) - sdk/typescript/src/auth/credential_provider.ts (new interface + tests) - sdk/python/redwood/subscribe.py (new facade + examples) - sdk/python/redwood/auth/credential_provider.py (new abstraction + tests) - sdk/go/subscriber/subscribe.go (new channel-based API) - sdk/go/cred/provider.go (new credential provider contract) - docs/streaming.md (rewritten to show subscribe + credential provider patterns) - examples/subscribe/ (updated multi-lang examples) - changelogs/python/1.31.0.md, changelogs/ts/1.27.0.md, changelogs/go/1.8.1.md Small illustrative snippet (TS): ```ts
// new subscribe convenience helper
for await (const msg of client.subscribe({route: 'chat'}) ) {
  // handle chunked events
}
``` Compatibility and migration notes: - `client.stream()` will continue to work but logs a deprecation warning; callsites should migrate to `client.subscribe()` before the next major version. - The new CredentialProvider interface is intentionally tiny; internal transport adapters provide a default provider that mirrors previous behavior. - No customer-facing server changes required; this is an SDK surface change only. Risk and mitigation: - Risk: Regressions in streaming reconnect/reauth paths. Mitigation: added integration tests that exercise token rotation mid-stream and a small canary to exercise long-running subscriptions during rollout. - Risk: Type/shape drift between SDKs. Mitigation: cross-language API contract doc and test vectors included. Rollout plan: - Merge to main, publish minor releases for each SDK in the same deployment window, monitor canary usage, follow up with deprecation schedule in docs and a support banner.
